A||||9||Urchin||Selling a car with a lean....(question)||||1||
Z||000000||Urchin||09-09-2001||09:48 PM||||I am currently financing a car (through Royal Bank) from the GM dealership.  Long story short...  I have run into financial difficulties and am considering getting rid of the car.  Being as I do not own it outright, would I have to return it to the dealer or can I sell it privately?  And if I could sell it privately would that mean that the money would go straight to Royal bank to account for the debt, or would I be able to use the money as I see fit?<BR>Thanks...<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000001||beer_stud_21||09-09-2001||09:54 PM||||I don't know much about this sort of thing.  But I know you can sell the car for a certain amount of cash, plus the new owners take over the payments.  This way you recoupe some of what you already paid for the car.  I dont think you can sell the car outright and then not pay off the loan, but I could be wrong.  The trick is to not buy brand new cars, especially when you cant afford it!<P>-marlin||||reg||1
Z||000002||RedRavenGT||09-09-2001||10:18 PM||||Heres what I would do:<P>Example:<P>Advertise as say, $500 plus take over payments?! Then you would have to sign the loan over to the new owner. Provided they had good enough credit.<P>Or I think you can sell at a certain price, pay off the loan and just let the bank give the new owner the title...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Urchin||09-09-2001||10:24 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by beer_stud_21:<BR><B>  The trick is to not buy brand new cars, especially when you cant afford it!<P>-marlin</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the tip  <BR>Just for the record, it was the result of an unexpected lay off... not something I'm happy about<p>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick's Dad||09-09-2001||10:45 PM||||I'd think that if the car leaned at all, I wouldn't want to buy it....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Seriously, the leinholder (Royal Bank) is the owner of the car, just as a bank or mortgage co. is (technically) owner of my home. You have a responsibilty to repay that lein, secured by the bank's ability to forclose (reposess) the collateral, regardless of whose yard it is in. This means that, if you sell the car and use the funds to feed your family, the bank will a) not provide you with the title to give to the buyers, and b) take the car back when you fail to pay the loan anymore. Banks are surprisingly humorless about this. Most buyers are also wary when you cannot provide a clear title.<P>If you can get more fundage than you owe on the car, then, of course, you only have to pay what you owe, and the rest of the money is yours. Once the note is satisified, the title will be sent by the bank to the buyers. In most states, a copy of the bill of sale (even handwritten) is valid for sales tax, registration, traffic infractions, etc. until the title appears a) at the buyer's home, or b) at the buyer's financial institution, if they had to borrow for it.<P>My wife had to deal with much of this when the Credit Union that she worked for was defrauded on a car loan and a personal loan by a scumbag that spent two years winning the trust of she and her coworkers.<P>Sorry to hear about your layoff. Hope that it is short-lived.||||reg||12
Z||000005||Tonker||09-09-2001||11:56 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Patrick's Dad:<BR><B>I'd think that if the car leaned at all, I wouldn't want to buy it....    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Sorry to hear about your layoff. Hope that it is short-lived.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Duh.. forgot to add my comments <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>1) Beat me too it there!<BR>2) I agree, in the same boat, just bought my Fiero on credit and got layed off.. not a happy feeling. I'm sure you'll find something though. A lot of the people I worked with have been having luck. It's time for me to start trying too.<p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroBUZZ||09-10-2001||12:13 AM||||Urchin:<BR>In Ontario, the new owner will have to have a 'Used Vehicle Package' bought (by you or him/her) to register the car.  It will show previous ownership history as well as any leins outstanding on the car.  If they have financed, their company should discover the lein and make any cheque payable to the Royal Bank.  Yes, financial institutions are notoriously fussy about such things.  If they are not paid off, they will track it down and repossess.<P>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||09-10-2001||11:21 AM||||Any time you have a "lein" on a car, that has to be paid off before ownership can transfer.  The car is the security for the loan, so if you no longer have the car, the loan is no longer secured.  When you sell, and you can sell privately, you have to get the money for the car, pay off the loan and get the title from the leinholder, then you can transfer the title from you to the new purchaser.  This can all be done at one time, if your buyers come with you to wherever the leinholder is.  <P>If you can't contact the bank in person, you'll have to send the money to the bank, have them send you the title, then you can have the title transferred to the new owners.  <P>The catch-22 a lot of new car owners have is they find out the amount the car will sell for is less than what they owe on the loan.  That means you have to come up with the balance yourself to pay it off.  If you owe $12K and sell the car for $10K, you've gotta come up with $2K out of your own pocket just to get the title transferred to the new owners.  A lot of dealers now say they'll pay off your trade no matter how much you owe.  All they're doing is adding that amount (called negative equity) to the amount you're borrowing for your new car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||09-10-2001||11:33 AM||||the lien is for the balance of the note..less the interest you wouldnt have to pay for paying it off early. You can sell the car for whatever you want, but you will not recieve the clear title until that balance is covered, anything over that is yours. The sell to take over payments is another option, but make sure whoever buys it does it thru the bank. If not (and they wont get a title) and they dont pay the bank will still come to you for the money.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ray b||09-10-2001||12:17 PM||||park it on a hill<BR>use the curb to adjust<BR>over/under inflate tires<BR>potholes well placed<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||6
